<Aside type="note" title="Use a validated toolchain, not an open-ended version range">
Use the versions pinned in the approved project template and its lockfiles. Confirm compatibility with the SDK catalog and the Machine Setup guide before upgrading. A language, framework, compiler, build tool, and SDK must work together as a supported set.
</Aside>

The source page listed Kotlin 1.9+, Swift 5.9+, React Native 0.73+, and TypeScript 5.0+. These ranges do not establish a current compatibility policy and should not be used as installation instructions. This overview intentionally defers version selection to the maintained project configuration.

### Platform conventions

| Concern | Native Android | Native iOS | React Native |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Application language | Kotlin | Swift | TypeScript, compiled to JavaScript |
| UI | Jetpack Compose | SwiftUI | React Native components |
| Presentation state | ViewModel exposes read-only `StateFlow` | Observable model with explicit ownership | Hooks and explicit state ownership |
| Async work | Coroutines and Flow | Structured concurrency with `async`/`await` | Promises and `async`/`await`; native modules for platform work |
| Dependency construction | Hilt | Initializer injection, protocols, and factories | Explicit dependencies, factories, and Context where appropriate |
| Local data | Room for structured data | Approved persistence adapter | Approved storage adapter for the data and query requirements |
| Sensitive credentials | Approved secure-storage SDK | Approved Keychain abstraction | Approved SDK backed by native secure storage |

**Runtime details matter:** Android applications execute on Android Runtime; a project's Java/JDK toolchain setting is a separate build concern. React Native executes JavaScript on its configured engine; Hermes does not execute TypeScript directly. SwiftUI's Observation integration requires supported OS versions—starting with iOS 17—so confirm the deployment target before prescribing `@Observable` throughout an app. See [Apple's model-data guidance](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/managing-model-data-in-your-app) and [React Native's Hermes guide](https://reactnative.dev/docs/hermes).

## Choose a platform

For an existing application, follow its established stack. For a new application, evaluate the required user experience, supported devices, native integrations, operational constraints, and the team's ability to maintain the result.

| Requirement | Native Android | Native iOS | React Native |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Android-only product or extensive Android APIs | Direct platform access; a strong starting point | Does not target Android | Assess the required native modules and maintenance cost |
| iOS-only product or extensive Apple APIs | Does not target iOS | Direct platform access; a strong starting point | Assess the required native modules and maintenance cost |
| Shared Android and iOS business workflows | Separate platform implementation | Separate platform implementation | A strong candidate when meaningful UI and logic can be shared |
| Specialized hardware, background work, or platform extensions | Assess Android API and device support | Assess Apple API and entitlement support | Validate native-module support and lifecycle behavior early |
| Strict performance requirements | Measure against the target workload | Measure against the target workload | Prototype critical interactions on representative devices |
| Existing product and team expertise | Include integration and ownership cost | Include integration and ownership cost | Include native engineering capacity as well as React expertise |

**Before committing:** Validate the highest-risk integration in a small prototype, record the trade-offs, and agree the choice with the responsible architecture and product owners. Shared code alone does not guarantee lower delivery or maintenance cost.

