### Responsibility boundaries

| Capability | Shared SDK responsibility | Application responsibility |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Identity and SSO | Approved OAuth/OIDC integration, session handling, and secure credential access | Trigger sign-in when required, react to session changes, and clear user-scoped state |
| Feature configuration | Retrieve and cache flags; expose defined fallback behavior | Provide safe feature defaults and render consistently while configuration changes |
| Observability | Approved event transport, diagnostics, and supported crash integration | Supply meaningful events, apply data-handling policy, and avoid credentials or sensitive payloads |
| Network resilience | Supported timeout, retry, circuit breaker, and outbox mechanisms | Define business-safe retry behavior, idempotency needs, and user-visible recovery |
| Design system | Brand tokens, supported themes, and shared UI primitives | Build accessible screens and use the approved components consistently |

The source architecture identifies Unleash Edge for flags and Datadog RUM for observability. Confirm the approved provider and available capabilities in the SDK catalog. Support for a circuit breaker, an outbox, or native crash capture must be checked per SDK and platform; the diagram does not guarantee that every adapter implements every capability.

<Aside type="caution" title="Secure storage is a platform capability">
Android Keystore manages cryptographic keys; it is not a general-purpose token database. Use the approved credential-storage abstraction, which can use Keystore-protected encryption where appropriate. On iOS, use the approved Keychain abstraction. React Native applications must use the native-backed secure-storage adapter rather than general-purpose storage for credentials. Hardware-backed protection depends on the device, key configuration, and platform capabilities; it must not be assumed universally.
</Aside>

See the [Android Keystore documentation](https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/keystore) for key-management behavior.

## Application startup

**Configure services early; keep remote work off the critical path unless the application actually requires it.** SDK construction, telemetry consent, session restoration, and remote configuration are separate concerns. Initializing an SDK does not mean initiating an interactive sign-in or waiting for every network request.

<MermaidZoom title="Startup lifecycle · reliable first render">

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    LAUNCH(["Application launch"]) --> CONFIG["Load validated environment configuration"]
    CONFIG --> VALID{"Required local<br/>configuration valid?"}
    VALID -->|"No"| BLOCK["Show controlled startup error<br/>Record safe diagnostics"]
    VALID -->|"Yes"| BUILD["Construct approved SDK dependencies<br/>Once per owning lifecycle"]
    BUILD --> LOCAL["Apply consent policy<br/>Load local session and flag state"]
    LOCAL --> SHELL["Render application shell<br/>Resolve access before protected content"]
    SHELL --> REMOTE["Refresh session and remote flags<br/>Asynchronously where supported"]
    REMOTE --> RESULT{"Refresh outcome"}
    RESULT -->|"Success"| READY["Publish updated application state"]
    RESULT -->|"Transient failure"| DEGRADED["Use permitted cached state or safe defaults<br/>Expose retry when needed"]
    RESULT -->|"Session invalid"| SIGNIN["Clear protected user state<br/>Request sign-in when required"]

    classDef entry fill:#0f172a,stroke:#475569,color:#ffffff,stroke-width:2px;
